// Chapter 10: growing a running cluster with storage nodes on new workers. It
// follows the model chapter's order: the discovery run narrowed to the new
// machines, the growth document it drafts and its review, what the expansion
// does, how the node adds are paced, and what happens to the data afterward.
// It also says what an expansion is not: adding a drive to an existing node,
// passing 32 storage nodes, and a rebalance the operator runs itself.
TRAINING.register({
  id: 'cluster-expansion',
  title: 'Expanding a running cluster',
  summary: 'Adding storage nodes to a cluster that is already serving: a second ClusterDeploymentConfig naming the cluster, drafted by a discovery run over the new workers, paced by the cluster\'s own node-add budget, and followed by the control plane spreading data onto the new nodes.',
  goal: 'a reader can grow a running cluster with a reviewed growth document, read the validation findings that refuse one, set the pace of the node adds on the cluster, keep failure domains balanced for a later removal, and say what moves the data afterward and what does not.',

  sections: [
    {
      heading: 'What growing a cluster means in this operator',
      body: [
        'A running cluster grows by gaining storage nodes on workers it does not use yet. The operator has one reviewed path for that: a second ClusterDeploymentConfig that names the existing cluster in `spec.clusterRef` and lists only the new workers. It is never an edit of the document that created the cluster. That document is immutable once approved, and the admission webhook refuses any change to its spec with a message that says to write a second config naming the cluster in `spec.clusterRef`.',
        'A growth document creates and never removes. A worker it lists that already has a storage node of the cluster produces nothing, and a node set left out of a later document drains nothing. Taking nodes out is a `StorageNodeOps` with action `Remove`, which [the node-removal chapter](chapter.html?c=node-removal) covers. A StorageNode written by hand is also admitted once its `clusterRef` resolves and its sizing matches the cluster, but it skips the review this chapter is about.',
        { table: { head: ['What is wanted', 'How it is done today'], rows: [
          ['Storage nodes on new workers', 'A growth ClusterDeploymentConfig with `spec.clusterRef`, usually drafted by a discovery run with `spec.discover.clusterRef`'],
          ['More node adds at once', '`StorageCluster.spec.storageNodes.nodeProvisioningBudget`, edited on the cluster'],
          ['Data spread onto the new nodes', 'The control plane, asked through each new node\'s `spec.config.expand` flag, and optionally a `StorageClusterOps` with action `Expand`'],
          ['A drive added to an existing node', 'No operation. The last part of the data section says what exists'],
          ['More than 32 storage nodes', 'Not by growing a standalone cluster. A supercluster, planned from creation, takes new members'],
        ] } },
        { callout: 'The commissioned framing was "Expanding a cluster". Three parts of it are taught as the code has them. Adding drives to an existing node is not an expansion the operator performs. A standalone cluster that reaches 32 storage nodes cannot be turned into a supercluster afterward. The operator has no rebalance action of its own, and what the control plane does with an expansion request is outside this repository, so the chapter describes the operator\'s side of the call only.', kind: 'info' },
        'The chapter follows one cluster, `prod` on OpenShift in the namespace `simplyblock`, with failure domains enabled and six storage nodes on `worker-01` to `worker-06`, two in each of the zones `zone-a`, `zone-b`, and `zone-c`. The growth adds `worker-07`, `worker-08`, and `worker-09`, one per zone.',
      ],
    },
    {
      heading: 'Discovery narrowed to the new workers',
      body: [
        'Label the new workers the way the existing ones are labeled, the failure-domain label included, and then run discovery against them alone. Two fields make a run a growth run. `spec.discover.clusterRef` names the cluster and is copied into the draft\'s `spec.clusterRef`. `spec.discover.workers` names the new machines, or `spec.discover.nodeSelector` selects them by label. The two are exclusive, and a run that states both is refused at admission.',
        'A run reports only unclaimed workers: a worker any StorageNode of the namespace already runs on is left out. Even an unnarrowed run against a deployed fleet therefore finds only machines without storage nodes. Narrowing still matters, because a worker added to the Kubernetes cluster for some other purpose has no storage node either, and an unnarrowed run would propose it.',
        'Because the cluster exists, the draft proposes no cluster layout. It has no `spec.cluster`, and the run\'s tolerations do not reach it. The run says so in a `ConfigWritten` event, `the draft grows the existing cluster prod, so it proposes no cluster layout`, and its `status.configRef` names the draft.',
        { code: 'oc apply -f 01-operatorops-discover-growth.yaml\noc get oops prod-grow-1 -n simplyblock -w\noc describe oops prod-grow-1 -n simplyblock\noc get cdc prod-growth-1 -n simplyblock -o wide', title: 'Running the growth discovery' },
        'The run reads each worker\'s failure domain from `topology.kubernetes.io/zone`, or from the label `spec.discover.failureDomainLabel` names. Use the label the cluster\'s first run used. A worker without it gets no failure domain in the draft, and a cluster with failure domains enabled does not accept a group without one.',
      ],
      examples: [
        { title: 'The discovery run for the growth', file: 'examples/cluster-expansion/01-operatorops-discover-growth.yaml', notes: [
          { at: 'clusterRef: prod', text: 'Makes the run a growth run. It is copied into the draft, and the draft gets no `spec.cluster`. The field is at most 63 characters, the length of a StorageCluster name.' },
          { at: 'configName: prod-growth-1', text: 'The draft\'s name. Without it the draft is named `discovered-<run name>`, so a second run never overwrites a draft somebody may have corrected.' },
          { at: 'workers:', text: 'The machines to probe, by node name. Exclusive with `nodeSelector`, and a named worker the run declines is reported by the same event a selected one would be.' },
          { at: 'enablePartitionedDevices: true', text: 'Reports disks with a stale partition table, which machines that held data before carry. A mounted or busy device is still never reported.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    {
      heading: 'Reviewing and approving the growth document',
      body: [
        'The draft holds `spec.clusterRef` and the new node sets, with each worker\'s interfaces, failure domain, and explicit device list. Some fields of a document are spent only when the document creates a cluster: `spec.environment`, `spec.hostOS`, `spec.cluster`, and the `nodeAgent` and `talosctl` slots of `spec.images`. On a growth document against a standalone cluster they change nothing, including a `nodeProvisioningBudget` written into `spec.cluster`. The `spdk` and `spdkProxy` image slots do reach the new nodes.',
        { steps: [
          'Read `status.message`. Validation runs on every pass and lists the first finding, with the count of the others, and each distinct reason is also a Warning event on the document.',
          'Check that every new worker is listed and that no existing one is. A worker the cluster already has a node on produces nothing new, whatever devices the group gives it.',
          'Check the devices. They are the class the cluster is built of, NVMe here, and the boot disk is not among them.',
          'Check that every group carries a `failureDomain`, one of the labels the cluster maps or a new one on purpose.',
          'Set `spec.approved: true`.',
        ] },
        'There are two checkpoints, and they do not check the same things. The approving edit is checked at admission against the live cluster: missing workers, the erasure-coding minimums, a `clusterRef` that names no cluster, and a device class other than the cluster\'s. Everything else is found by the controller, and an approved document with any finding goes `Failed` rather than waiting, because approval made it immutable and the finding can no longer be edited away. The 32-node cap and the failure-domain rule are among the checks the webhook does not repeat, so a draft is approved only once its `status.message` says it is valid and waiting for `spec.approved`.',
        { table: { head: ['Reason', 'What it means for a growth document'], rows: [
          ['`WorkerNotFound`', 'A listed worker is not a Kubernetes node, a worker appears in two groups, or the groups name different interfaces'],
          ['`DeviceNotFound`', 'A group names no devices. The missing-failure-domain finding is reported under this reason as well'],
          ['`DeviceClassMismatch`', 'The groups name a device class other than the cluster\'s `spec.deviceClass`, where unset means NVMe'],
          ['`TooManyFailureDomains`', 'The document\'s labels and the ones the cluster already maps come to more than 256'],
          ['`StripeBelowMinimumNodes`, `StripeBelowMinimumWorkers`, `StripeSingleNodeCount`', 'The cluster\'s stripe, counted over the nodes it has and the nodes the document adds'],
          ['`MemberTooLarge`', 'The cluster\'s nodes and the new ones come to more than 32, or one node set alone makes more than 32'],
          ['`MemberSpansRegions`', 'One node set lists workers in more than one `topology.kubernetes.io/region`'],
          ['`NoManagementInterface`', 'A group names no `mgmtInterface`'],
        ] } },
        'The 32-node cap is counted in storage nodes, not workers. Each worker gets one node per slot, and the slots come from the cluster\'s `socketsToUse` and `nodesPerSocket`, so a two-socket fleet reaches the cap at 16 workers. The existing count is every StorageNode object that names the cluster on this site, whatever its phase, so a removed node whose object was never deleted still counts. A document over the cap is held whole, with this message:',
        { code: 'the document adds 3 storage nodes to cluster prod, which has 30, and a cluster holds at most 32', title: 'The MemberTooLarge message for a growth document' },
        'The cap exists because a cluster\'s fault tolerance is its stripe\'s and does not grow with the node count, while the chance that more nodes fail at once than the stripe tolerates does. Past it, a standalone cluster has no way forward: the StorageCluster schema refuses turning a cluster into a supercluster after creation. A deployment expected to pass 32 nodes is created as a supercluster from the start, and a growth document naming the supercluster then adds a member, which [the placement-groups chapter](chapter.html?c=openshift-placement-groups) covers. For a standalone cluster at its cap, the next capacity is a second cluster from its own document, with its own pool and StorageClass.',
        'The stripe minimums rarely bite on growth, since the cluster already met them. The exception is a single-node cluster: `clusterMode: Single` runs exactly one storage node, the schema refuses changing the mode to or from `Single`, and every growth document against such a cluster is refused with `StripeSingleNodeCount`.',
      ],
      examples: [
        { title: 'The growth document as drafted', file: 'examples/cluster-expansion/02-growth-draft.yaml', notes: [
          { at: 'approved: false', text: 'A draft is validated on every pass and expanded on none. Nothing is created from it until this is true.' },
          { at: 'environment: OpenShift', text: 'Detected by the run and written into every draft. A growth document against a standalone cluster does not spend it, since the cluster\'s workload is already built.' },
          { at: 'clusterRef: prod', text: 'The cluster to add to. There is no `spec.cluster`: prod\'s sizing, stripe, and failure-domain mode are read from the live object.' },
          { at: 'name: discovered', text: 'The node set name discovery gives every draft. The StorageNodes record it in `spec.nodeSet`, which is why a reviewer may rename it.' },
          { at: 'failureDomain: zone-a', text: 'Read from the worker\'s zone label. prod has failure domains enabled, so every group needs one.' },
        ] },
        { title: 'The growth document as approved', file: 'examples/cluster-expansion/03-growth-approved.yaml', notes: [
          { at: 'approved: true', text: 'The approving edit is checked at admission against the live cluster. From here the spec is immutable and approval cannot be withdrawn.' },
          { at: 'name: growth-2026-11', text: 'Renamed in review, so each new StorageNode records which growth produced it.' },
          { at: 'worker-08', text: 'One worker per zone. Validation does not check the balance of a growth, and the failure-domain section says why it still matters.' },
          { at: '0000:c1:00.0', text: 'Each device by PCI address, of the class prod is built of. A block-device path here would be refused with `DeviceClassMismatch`.' },
        ] },
        { title: 'A growth document held by the 32-node cap', file: 'examples/cluster-expansion/04-growth-over-cap.yaml', notes: [
          { at: 'name: prod-growth-4', text: 'Written later in prod\'s life, when it has 30 storage nodes. The schema admits the document.' },
          { at: 'approved: false', text: 'Held as a draft with `MemberTooLarge`. Approved as it stands, it would be admitted and then go `Failed`, since the webhook does not check the cap.' },
          { at: 'worker-33', text: 'The third worker is the one too many. 30 existing nodes and 3 new ones make 33.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    {
      heading: 'What the expansion does',
      body: [
        'Once approved, the document moves `Draft`, `Expanding`, and then `Expanded` or `Failed`, and its `status.step` walks five steps. An approved document waits in `Expanding` with a `ControlPlaneNotReady` event while the ControlPlane is neither `Available` nor `Degraded`.',
        { table: { head: ['Step', 'Budget', 'What it does on a growth document'], rows: [
          ['`Validating`', '5 minutes', 'Nothing more: validation already ran and found nothing'],
          ['`CreatingCluster`', '5 minutes', 'Resolves the existing cluster and records it in `status.clusterRef`. A `clusterRef` naming nothing fails the document with `ClusterNotFound`'],
          ['`AwaitingCluster`', '30 minutes', 'Waits for the cluster\'s `status.uuid`, which a running cluster already has'],
          ['`CreatingNodes`', '10 minutes', 'Maps new failure-domain labels into the cluster\'s `status.failureDomains`, then creates one StorageNode per worker per slot that does not exist yet, each with `spec.config.expand: true`'],
          ['`Activating`', '60 minutes', 'Waits for every node it created to be `Online`, raising `AwaitingNodes`, then creates the StorageClusterOps `<cluster>-activate` or finds it already there'],
        ] } },
        'The last step is the same one a creating document runs, and on a running cluster it asks for nothing new. The activation operation of the original deployment usually still exists, which counts as done. If it was deleted, a new one is created, and an `Activate` skips its call for a cluster that is already active. The growth document never raises a `StorageClusterOps` with action `Expand`.',
        'The new StorageNodes carry the labels `storage.simplyblock.io/cluster` and `storage.simplyblock.io/worker`, and their sizing is copied from the cluster. On success the document records the nodes in `status.nodeRefs` and raises `NodesCreated` with `expanded into cluster prod and 3 node(s)`. Nothing refers back to the document, so deleting it later deletes no node.',
        { code: 'oc get cdc prod-growth-1 -n simplyblock -w\noc get sn -n simplyblock -l storage.simplyblock.io/cluster=prod\noc get events -n simplyblock --field-selector involvedObject.name=prod-growth-1', title: 'Following the expansion' },
        { callout: 'The `Activating` step allows 60 minutes for every node the document created to come `Online`. A growth that adds its nodes more slowly than that, such as many OpenShift workers at a budget of one, leaves the document `Failed` with `step Activating outlived its deadline` and a `StepDeadlineExceeded` event. The nodes are not affected and keep provisioning: the document is only the record.', kind: 'warn' },
      ],
    },
    {
      heading: 'Pacing: the cluster\'s budget and FoundationDB',
      body: [
        'Each new StorageNode walks its own provisioning steps: `CheckingHost`, `CheckingConfig`, `CheckingNetwork` where the cluster declares networks, `AwaitingSlot`, `Posting`, and `Resolving`, with `AwaitingWorker` while the worker reboots. The node\'s phase moves from `Pending` through `Provisioning` to `Online`. On OpenShift a worker\'s first node add applies a machine config and reboots it, and a note in the operator\'s code records eleven minutes for one such cordon, drain, reboot, and uncordon.',
        '`AwaitingSlot` is the pace. The cap is the cluster\'s `spec.storageNodes.nodeProvisioningBudget`, 1 by default, counted by distinct worker, so a two-socket worker takes one slot. The slots held are listed in the cluster\'s `status.provisioningSlots`, and a waiting node raises `AwaitingSlot` with `waiting for a node-add slot, 1 of 1 in flight` and the workers holding them. A slot is kept across the worker\'s reboot.',
        'A growth document does not change the budget of the cluster it grows. The budget in `spec.cluster` of a document reaches only a cluster that document creates, so the budget is edited on the StorageCluster, before or during the growth. Nodes still waiting see the new value on their next pass.',
        { code: 'oc patch stc prod -n simplyblock --type merge -p \'{"spec":{"storageNodes":{"nodeProvisioningBudget":3}}}\'\noc get stc prod -n simplyblock -o jsonpath=\'{.status.provisioningSlots}\'', title: 'Raising the budget on the cluster' },
        'Workers that host a FoundationDB pod are added one at a time whatever the budget says. A node add reboots its host, and two FoundationDB hosts rebooting together reduce the control plane\'s own fault tolerance. A FoundationDB worker that has to wait raises `AwaitingSlot` with `worker worker-08 hosts FoundationDB and worker worker-07 is already being added`. The rule is in the slot logic, and the repository has no end-to-end test of it on a cluster.',
        'A node may wait in `AwaitingSlot` for four hours. Past that it goes `Failed` with `step AwaitingSlot outlived its deadline`, so a large growth at a budget of one is run with a higher budget or as several documents approved one after another.',
      ],
    },
    {
      heading: 'Failure domains when growing',
      body: [
        'The failure-domain mode is the cluster\'s, immutable, and read from the live StorageCluster, so a growth document restates nothing about it. Every group still needs a `failureDomain`. A document missing one gets a validation finding, and a StorageNode without one on such a cluster holds in `CheckingConfig` with the event `FailureDomainMissing`. A new label is given the next free index in the cluster\'s `status.failureDomains` during `CreatingNodes`, and an entry once written is never changed.',
        'Validation does not check the balance of a growth. The layout rule for activation, equal workers per domain and at least the stripe\'s parity plus two domains, applies only to a document that creates its cluster, and the `Activate` operation\'s failure-domain gate applies only to a cluster that is not active yet.',
        'The balance is still enforced, at each add. According to the operator\'s code, the control plane refuses an expansion add that leaves the domains more than one host apart, and it does so inside the add task, after the request was accepted. The control plane\'s side of that rule is not in this repository. The operator orders the waiting workers so each free slot goes to the domain with the fewest hosts. One worker per domain therefore adds cleanly. Three workers in one domain do not: the second is refused inside its task and retried while it keeps its slot, and at a budget of one nothing else is added until its step runs out.',
        'Balanced growth also keeps every node removable later. A `Remove` operation is held in `Validating` with `RemovalNotAdmitted` unless, after the removal, the domains differ by at most one host and every domain keeps at least two. A cluster at 3, 3, and 2 hosts cannot give up a node of the domain holding 2, since that domain would drop to one host. Adding in whole rounds of one worker per domain is what keeps a later removal possible, and [the node-removal chapter](chapter.html?c=node-removal) covers the removal itself.',
      ],
    },
    {
      heading: 'What happens to the data, and to drives on existing nodes',
      body: [
        'Every node a growth document creates carries `spec.config.expand: true`, immutable, and the node add sends it to the control plane. The operator\'s types describe the flag as a request to rebalance onto the new node rather than to treat it as part of an initial layout. How and when the control plane moves data is not in this repository.',
        'The cluster\'s phase shows a rebalance. While the control plane reports `rebalancing`, the phase reads `Rebalancing` in place of `Online` or `Degraded`, and `status.status` keeps the control plane\'s own word beside it. A control-plane status of `in_expansion` reads as `Provisioning`, and a removal in progress reads as `Shrinking` over both. While the cluster rebalances, a `StorageNodeOps` with action `Migrate` or `HostMaintenance` waits with `ClusterNotReady`.',
        'A `StorageClusterOps` with action `Expand` is the optional explicit request. Nothing raises one. It posts to the control plane\'s expand endpoint with no body in `Requesting`, which allows two minutes and can be aborted, and then waits in `Awaiting`, for up to 30 minutes, for the cluster to read active. It has no state to skip on, so every `Expand` written posts again: write one after the last new node is `Online`, not one per node. Whether an expansion should imply a rebalance is an open question in the StorageCluster design, which records that today it happens whenever the rebalancer next runs.',
        'The auto-rebalancer is opt-in, with `spec.enableVolumeAutoPlacement: true` on the StorageCluster. It moves volumes between nodes as migrations, driven by measured latency, and `spec.volumeAutoPlacement.disableMigration` runs it as a dry run. Off, which is the default, it moves nothing.',
        'Data realignment is a different thing. After volumes move, by the auto-rebalancer, a manual migration, or a drain, the operator asks the control plane to realign its internal structures to where the volumes now are. It moves no volume. It is on by default and turned off with `spec.disableDataRealignment: true`. It runs once `status.volumeMoveGeneration` is at least `minMoves` ahead of `status.realignedGeneration`, at least `interval` after `status.lastDataRealignmentAt`, and while no volume is moving. The defaults are 1 move and 10 minutes, set in `spec.volumeMigrationSettings.dataRealignment`. Its events on the StorageCluster are `DataRealignmentTriggered` and `DataRealignmentFailed`. A growth alone moves no volume through a migration, so it raises nothing to realign.',
        'A realignment is forced with the annotation `simplyblock.io/trigger-realignment` on the StorageCluster, with any value that is not empty. It skips the interval, the move count, and the wait for moving volumes, and the operator removes it once the request succeeds. The controller reads this spelling only. The StorageCluster design document writes it with a `storage.simplyblock.io/` prefix, which the controller ignores, and with realignment turned off the annotation does nothing.',
        { code: 'oc annotate stc prod simplyblock.io/trigger-realignment=now -n simplyblock', title: 'Forcing a realignment' },
        'Drives are not added to an existing node through any operation. `StorageDeviceOps` has the actions `Restart`, `Fail`, and `CheckHealth`, and no `Add`. A node\'s `spec.config`, its `deviceNames` included, can be changed by the operator alone, and a growth document creates nothing on a worker the cluster already has a node on. The StorageDevice objects mirror what the control plane reports for each node, so a drive the control plane takes into a node appears as a new StorageDevice. Whether and when the control plane picks up a drive inserted into a running node is control-plane behavior outside this repository.',
      ],
      examples: [
        { title: 'An explicit expansion request', file: 'examples/cluster-expansion/05-storageclusterops-expand.yaml', notes: [
          { at: 'clusterRef: prod', text: 'The cluster to act on. Immutable, and the operation never owns the cluster.' },
          { at: 'action: Expand', text: 'Posts to the control plane\'s expand endpoint, then waits for the cluster to read active. No parameters exist for it.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    {
      heading: 'What goes wrong',
      body: [
        { pitfalls: [
          { symptom: 'Editing the approved document that created the cluster is refused with `spec is immutable once spec.approved is true`.', cause: 'An approved document is the record of one deployment and cannot change.', fix: 'Write a second document with `spec.clusterRef` naming the cluster and only the new workers.' },
          { symptom: 'The growth draft holds with `MemberTooLarge`, naming the nodes it adds and the nodes the cluster has.', cause: 'The cluster\'s existing nodes and the new ones come to more than 32. Objects of removed nodes count until they are deleted.', fix: 'List fewer workers, or plan the capacity as a second cluster. A standalone cluster cannot become a supercluster, and what deleting the object of a `Removed` node does is not covered here.' },
          { symptom: 'An approved growth document goes `Failed` straight away with a validation message.', cause: 'The webhook checks only workers, stripe minimums, the cluster reference, and the device class at approval. Any other finding fails an approved document.', fix: 'Delete the failed document and write it again, approving only once `status.message` says the document is valid.' },
          { symptom: 'The new nodes sit in `AwaitingSlot` one after another.', cause: 'The cluster\'s `nodeProvisioningBudget` is 1, or the workers host FoundationDB, which is always sequential.', fix: 'Raise the budget on the StorageCluster. A budget in the growth document\'s `spec.cluster` is ignored.' },
          { symptom: 'The growth document goes `Failed` with `step Activating outlived its deadline` while its nodes are still provisioning.', cause: 'The nodes took longer than the step\'s 60 minutes to come `Online`.', fix: 'Nothing for the nodes, which continue. Use a higher budget or smaller documents next time.' },
          { symptom: 'A second new worker in one domain stays in its add, and the other new workers wait behind it.', cause: 'The growth was unbalanced, and the control plane refuses an add that leaves the domains more than one host apart.', fix: 'Grow one worker per domain per round. Validation does not check this for growth.' },
          { symptom: 'The discovery run proposes a machine that was never meant for storage.', cause: 'The run was not narrowed, and the machine has no storage node, so it counts as unclaimed.', fix: 'Name the new workers in `spec.discover.workers`, or remove the group from the draft before approving.' },
          { symptom: 'The new nodes are `Online` and the existing nodes still hold all the data.', cause: 'The operator does not move data itself. The control plane acts on the `expand` flag in its own time.', fix: 'Watch the cluster\'s phase for `Rebalancing`, write one `StorageClusterOps` `Expand` after the last node is `Online` if a request is wanted, or turn on `enableVolumeAutoPlacement`.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
  ],

  questions: [
    { topic: 'Growth document', cat: 'concept', text: 'How does this operator add storage nodes on new workers to a running cluster?', options: ['A second ClusterDeploymentConfig naming the cluster in `spec.clusterRef`', 'Editing `nodeSets` of the approved document that created the cluster', 'A `StorageClusterOps` with action `Expand` naming the new workers', 'Raising a node count on the StorageCluster', 'A `StorageNodeOps` with action `Add`'], correct: [0], explain: 'Growth is a second document. The first is immutable once approved, `Expand` takes no parameters, the StorageCluster has no node count, and `StorageNodeOps` has no `Add` action.' },
    { topic: 'Growth document', cat: 'concept', text: 'A later growth document lists `worker-03`, which already has a storage node of the cluster, with a new device list. What happens to that worker?', options: ['Nothing new is created for it', 'Its node\'s device list is replaced', 'The node is drained and rebuilt with the new devices', 'The document is refused with `ClusterExists`', 'A second node is created on another slot'], correct: [0], explain: 'The expansion creates only slots that do not exist. An existing node is listed in `status.nodeRefs` but not changed, and its `spec.config` can be changed by the operator alone.' },
    { topic: 'Growth document', cat: 'field', text: 'Which fields of a growth document against a standalone cluster change nothing?', options: ['`spec.environment`', '`spec.cluster.nodeProvisioningBudget`', '`spec.images.nodeAgent`', '`spec.images.spdk`', '`spec.nodeSets[].groups[].devices`'], correct: [0, 1, 2], explain: 'The environment, the cluster template, and the node-agent image are spent only when a document creates its cluster. The SPDK image and the devices are written onto each new node.' },
    { topic: 'Growth document', cat: 'concept', text: 'A node set is left out of a later growth document. What does the operator do to the nodes that set produced earlier?', options: ['Drains them', 'Removes them from the cluster', 'Marks them `Degraded`', 'Moves them into the new node set', 'Raises a `StorageNodeOps` `Remove` for each'], correct: [], explain: 'None of these. A document creates or adds and never removes. Removal is a deliberate `StorageNodeOps` `Remove`.' },
    { topic: 'Growth document', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which statements about deleting a growth document after it has expanded are true?', options: ['The nodes it created stay', 'Nothing refers back to the document', 'The nodes it created are drained', 'The cluster returns to its earlier size', 'The cluster is deleted with it'], correct: [0, 1], explain: 'The document owns none of the nodes or the cluster. They are self-describing, so the document can be deleted without effect on them.' },

    { topic: 'Discovery', cat: 'field', text: 'Which fields make a discovery run write a growth document limited to three new machines?', options: ['`spec.discover.clusterRef`', '`spec.discover.workers`', '`spec.discover.enableControlPlaneNodes`', '`spec.discover.configName`', '`spec.discover.enableFailureDomains`'], correct: [0, 1], explain: '`clusterRef` makes the draft a growth document and `workers` limits the probes. `configName` only names the draft, and the other two decide other things.' },
    { topic: 'Discovery', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which statements about a discovery run with `spec.discover.clusterRef` are true?', options: ['The draft has no `spec.cluster`', 'The run\'s tolerations are not written into the draft', 'The draft is written with `approved: false`', 'A `ConfigWritten` event says the draft proposes no cluster layout', 'The draft is approved automatically when it validates'], correct: [0, 1, 2, 3], explain: 'The cluster exists, so no layout is proposed and no tolerations are carried. Every draft is written unapproved, and only a person approves one.' },
    { topic: 'Discovery', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which workers does any discovery run leave out?', options: ['Workers a StorageNode of the namespace already runs on', 'Workers without NVMe devices of a minimum size', 'Workers in another zone than the cluster', 'Workers added to Kubernetes after the cluster was created', 'Workers carrying the failure-domain label'], correct: [0], explain: 'A run reports only unclaimed workers. That is why a run against a deployed fleet finds the new machines without being narrowed.' },
    { topic: 'Discovery', cat: 'field', text: 'A run states both `workers` and `nodeSelector`. What happens?', options: ['It is refused at admission', 'The two are intersected', 'The names win over the selector', 'The selector wins over the names', 'The run probes the union of both'], correct: [0], explain: 'The two are exclusive, and a CEL rule on the discover block refuses a run that carries both.' },
    { topic: 'Discovery', cat: 'action', text: 'Where does a reviewer find which draft a growth run wrote?', options: ['The run\'s `status.configRef`', 'A `ConfigWritten` event on the run', 'The cluster\'s `status.nodeRefs`', 'The draft\'s `spec.approved`', 'The cluster\'s `status.provisioningSlots`'], correct: [0, 1], explain: 'The run records the draft by name and raises `ConfigWritten` events naming it. The other fields belong to other objects or say something else.' },

    { topic: 'Validation', cat: 'concept', text: 'A cluster has 30 storage nodes, one per worker, and a growth draft adds 3 workers. What does the draft show?', options: ['`MemberTooLarge` with `the document adds 3 storage nodes to cluster prod, which has 30, and a cluster holds at most 32`', 'Two workers added and the third refused', 'A refusal at admission of the draft itself', 'Nothing, the cap applies to new clusters only', '`TooManyFailureDomains`'], correct: [0], explain: 'Growth counts the nodes the cluster has and the nodes the document adds, and the document is held whole. A draft is admitted on its structure alone.' },
    { topic: 'Validation', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which of these count toward the 32-node cap of a growth document?', options: ['Every slot of every new worker', 'StorageNode objects of the cluster in phase `Removed` that were not deleted', 'Every StorageNode object of the cluster on this site', 'Workers of the cluster\'s FoundationDB that host no storage node', 'Remote nodes of a stretched cluster\'s other site'], correct: [0, 1, 2], explain: 'The count is in storage nodes, from the StorageNode objects naming the cluster whatever their phase, skipping remote nodes. Workers without storage nodes are not counted.' },
    { topic: 'Validation', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which checks does the admission webhook make on the edit that approves a growth document?', options: ['Every listed worker is a Kubernetes node', 'The stripe minimums, counted with the existing nodes', 'The `clusterRef` names an existing cluster', 'The device class matches the cluster\'s', 'The 32-node cap'], correct: [0, 1, 2, 3], explain: 'The webhook answers four questions against the live cluster. The cap is the controller\'s alone, which is why an approved document over it is admitted and then fails.' },
    { topic: 'Validation', cat: 'concept', text: 'An approved growth document has a validation finding the webhook did not check. What happens?', options: ['The document goes `Failed`', 'The document waits in `Draft` until the finding is fixed', 'The operator removes the offending group and expands the rest', 'Approval is withdrawn', 'The finding becomes a warning and the expansion continues'], correct: [0], explain: 'Approval made the document immutable, so a finding found after it cannot be edited away, and failing says more than waiting.' },
    { topic: 'Validation', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which ways forward exist for a standalone cluster that has reached 32 storage nodes?', options: ['A second cluster from its own document', 'Setting `spec.supercluster` on the existing cluster', 'A growth document naming the cluster with node sets of fewer workers', 'Raising the cap on the StorageCluster', 'A growth document with `spec.cluster` stating a larger cap'], correct: [0], explain: 'A cluster cannot become a supercluster after creation, the cap is a constant in the operator, and any document adding to the cluster is held by it.' },
    { topic: 'Validation', cat: 'concept', text: 'A growth document names a cluster with `clusterMode: Single`. What happens?', options: ['It is refused with `StripeSingleNodeCount`', 'The cluster switches to `Standard`', 'The new nodes become replicas of the single node', 'It is admitted and the nodes are added', 'The cluster becomes a two-node cluster'], correct: [0], explain: 'A single-node cluster runs exactly one storage node, and the schema refuses changing the mode to or from `Single`.' },

    { topic: 'Expansion steps', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which steps does a growth document walk after approval?', options: ['`Validating`', '`CreatingCluster`', '`AwaitingCluster`', '`CreatingNodes`', '`Activating`'], correct: [0, 1, 2, 3, 4], explain: 'Growth walks the same five steps as a creating document. On a running cluster, `CreatingCluster` resolves the cluster and `AwaitingCluster` finds its UUID already set.' },
    { topic: 'Expansion steps', cat: 'field', text: 'What does `CreatingNodes` write onto each StorageNode it creates for a growth document?', options: ['`spec.config.expand: true`', 'The cluster\'s `vcpuCount` and `minHugePagesSize` as the node\'s sizing', 'The group\'s device list as `spec.config.deviceNames`', 'An owner reference to the document', 'The document\'s `nodeProvisioningBudget`'], correct: [0, 1, 2], explain: 'The flag marks the node as an addition, the sizing is copied from the cluster, and the devices come from the group. Nothing on the node refers back to the document.' },
    { topic: 'Expansion steps', cat: 'concept', text: 'What does the `Activating` step of a growth document do on an active cluster?', options: ['Waits for every node it created to be `Online`', 'Finds or creates the StorageClusterOps `<cluster>-activate`', 'Raises a `StorageClusterOps` with action `Expand`', 'Reactivates the cluster, taking it offline briefly', 'Starts a rebalance of the cluster'], correct: [0, 1], explain: 'The step waits for its nodes and then asks for an activation, which an `Activate` skips for an active cluster. It never raises an `Expand`.' },
    { topic: 'Expansion steps', cat: 'concept', text: 'A growth document goes `Failed` with `step Activating outlived its deadline`. Which statements are true?', options: ['Its nodes took longer than 60 minutes to come `Online`', 'Its nodes keep provisioning', 'Its nodes are removed', 'The cluster is marked `Unavailable`', 'The document can be edited and retried'], correct: [0, 1], explain: 'The step allows 60 minutes, and the document failing does not touch the nodes. A `Failed` document is terminal and immutable.' },
    { topic: 'Expansion steps', cat: 'concept', text: 'What holds an approved growth document in `Expanding` with a `ControlPlaneNotReady` event?', options: ['A ControlPlane that is neither `Available` nor `Degraded`', 'A cluster in phase `Rebalancing`', 'A ControlPlane in phase `Degraded`', 'A node of another growth still provisioning', 'A ControlPlane named `simplyblock` that cannot be read'], correct: [0, 4], explain: 'The expansion needs a control plane that answers, and one it cannot read or one reporting another phase does not. `Degraded` answers, so it does not hold the document.' },

    { topic: 'Pacing', cat: 'field', text: 'Where does the node-add pacing of a growth come from?', options: ['`StorageCluster.spec.storageNodes.nodeProvisioningBudget`', '`spec.cluster.nodeProvisioningBudget` of the growth document', '`spec.discover.nodeProvisioningBudget` of the run', 'A label on each new worker', '`StorageClusterOps` `Expand`'], correct: [0], explain: 'The slot logic reads the cluster\'s field. A document\'s budget reaches only a cluster that document creates, and the other places do not exist.' },
    { topic: 'Pacing', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which statements about the node-add budget are true?', options: ['It is 1 by default', 'It counts distinct workers, so a two-socket worker takes one slot', 'The slots held are in the cluster\'s `status.provisioningSlots`', 'Workers hosting FoundationDB are added one at a time whatever it says', 'A slot is given up while the worker reboots'], correct: [0, 1, 2, 3], explain: 'The slot is kept across the reboot, which keeps the cap closed while a machine is down for its machine config.' },
    { topic: 'Pacing', cat: 'action', text: 'Ten new workers wait in `AwaitingSlot` at a budget of 1. Which actions speed up the growth?', options: ['Patching `spec.storageNodes.nodeProvisioningBudget` on the StorageCluster', 'Editing the approved growth document\'s `spec.cluster`', 'Writing a `StorageClusterOps` `Expand`', 'Deleting the growth document', 'Restarting the operator'], correct: [0], explain: 'Waiting nodes read the cluster\'s budget again on each pass. The approved document is immutable and its template is ignored for growth anyway.' },
    { topic: 'Pacing', cat: 'concept', text: 'What happens to a new node that waits in `AwaitingSlot` for more than four hours?', options: ['It goes `Failed` with `step AwaitingSlot outlived its deadline`', 'It takes a slot over the budget', 'It is deleted and recreated', 'It moves on to `Posting` regardless', 'The budget is raised automatically'], correct: [0], explain: 'The step has a four-hour deadline, which is why a large growth at a budget of one is split or run with a higher budget.' },

    { topic: 'Failure domains', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which of these apply to a growth document against a cluster with failure domains enabled?', options: ['Every group needs a `failureDomain`', 'A new label is mapped to the next free index in `status.failureDomains`', 'Equal worker counts per domain are checked at validation', 'The document restates `enableFailureDomains`', 'At least parity plus two domains are checked at validation'], correct: [0, 1], explain: 'The mode is read from the live cluster, and the activation layout rule applies only to a document creating its cluster.' },
    { topic: 'Failure domains', cat: 'concept', text: 'A growth adds three workers to `zone-a` of a cluster at 2, 2, and 2 hosts. What does the operator\'s code say happens?', options: ['The first adds, and the second is refused by the control plane inside its add task', 'Validation refuses the document', 'All three add, and the cluster rebalances domains afterward', 'The operator moves one worker to another zone', 'The webhook refuses the approval'], correct: [0], explain: 'The control plane refuses an add that leaves the domains more than one host apart, after the request was accepted. Nothing before that checks a growth\'s balance.' },
    { topic: 'Failure domains', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which conditions must hold after a removal for a `Remove` operation to be admitted on a cluster with failure domains?', options: ['The domains differ by at most one host', 'Every domain keeps at least two hosts', 'Every domain holds the same number of hosts', 'The removed node is in the largest domain', 'The cluster is `Online` and not rebalancing'], correct: [0, 1], explain: 'The operator holds the removal in `Validating` with `RemovalNotAdmitted` otherwise. Equality is not required, and `Remove` is exempt from the cluster gate.' },
    { topic: 'Failure domains', cat: 'action', text: 'Which growth plans for a cluster at 2, 2, and 2 hosts in three zones keep every node removable afterward?', options: ['One worker in each zone', 'Two workers in each zone, added one round at a time', 'Three workers in one zone', 'One worker in one zone and two in another', 'A new zone with one worker'], correct: [0, 1], explain: 'Whole rounds keep the domains within one host and every domain at two or more. A new zone with one host leaves that domain below two, and the removal rule then refuses every removal in the cluster.' },

    { topic: 'Data afterward', cat: 'concept', text: 'What moves data onto the new nodes of a growth?', options: ['The control plane, acting on each node\'s `expand` flag in its own time', 'The growth document\'s `Activating` step', 'A `StoragePoolOps` `Rebalance` the operator raises', 'Data realignment', 'The operator copying volumes at node add'], correct: [0], explain: 'The operator sends the flag and has no rebalance of its own. `StoragePoolOps` `Rebalance` is declared and refused at run time, and realignment moves no volume.' },
    { topic: 'Data afterward', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which statements about a `StorageClusterOps` with action `Expand` are true?', options: ['Nothing in the operator raises one', 'It posts to the expand endpoint with no body', 'It has no state to skip on, so each one posts again', 'It waits for the cluster to read active', 'It takes the new workers as parameters'], correct: [0, 1, 2, 3], explain: 'It is an optional, explicit request. It takes no parameters, and whether it implies a rebalance is an open question in the design.' },
    { topic: 'Data afterward', cat: 'field', text: 'Which field turns on the latency-driven auto-rebalancer?', options: ['`spec.enableVolumeAutoPlacement`', '`spec.disableDataRealignment`', '`spec.volumeMigrationSettings.dataRealignment.minMoves`', '`spec.storageNodes.nodeProvisioningBudget`', '`spec.config.expand`'], correct: [0], explain: 'The auto-rebalancer is opt-in and off by default. The others govern realignment, pacing, and how a node joined.' },
    { topic: 'Data afterward', cat: 'field', text: 'Which annotation forces a data realignment, spelled as the controller reads it?', options: ['`simplyblock.io/trigger-realignment`', '`storage.simplyblock.io/trigger-realignment`', '`simplyblock.io/realign`', '`storage.simplyblock.io/rebalance`', '`simplyblock.io/expand`'], correct: [0], explain: 'The controller reads the bare `simplyblock.io/` spelling. The design document\'s `storage.simplyblock.io/` spelling is ignored.' },
    { topic: 'Data afterward', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which statements about data realignment are true?', options: ['It moves volumes between nodes', 'It is off unless turned on', 'A growth by itself triggers it', 'It runs while volumes are still moving', 'The trigger annotation works with `disableDataRealignment: true`'], correct: [], explain: 'None of them. It realigns the control plane\'s structures and moves no volume, it is on by default, it follows volume moves rather than node adds, it waits for moving volumes unless forced, and it does nothing when turned off.' },
    { topic: 'Data afterward', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which of these are how the operator maps the control plane\'s report onto the StorageCluster\'s phase?', options: ['An active cluster with a rebalance running reads `Rebalancing`', 'A status of `in_expansion` reads `Provisioning`', 'A status of `in_activation` reads `Activating`', 'A cluster with new nodes provisioning reads `Expanding`', 'A rebalance during a removal reads `Rebalancing`'], correct: [0, 1, 2], explain: '`Expanding` is a phase of the ClusterDeploymentConfig, not of the cluster, and a removal in progress reads `Shrinking` over a rebalance.' },
    { topic: 'Data afterward', cat: 'action', text: 'A new drive is inserted into a worker that already runs a storage node. Which of these exist to hand it to the node?', options: ['A `StorageDeviceOps` with action `Add`', 'Editing the node\'s `spec.config.deviceNames`', 'A growth document listing the worker with the new device', 'Editing the node\'s `spec.config.pcieAllowList`', 'A `StorageNodeOps` with action `Add`'], correct: [], explain: 'None of them. `StorageDeviceOps` has `Restart`, `Fail`, and `CheckHealth`, a node\'s `spec.config` is the operator\'s to write, a growth document creates nothing on a worker with a node, and `StorageNodeOps` has no `Add`. A drive the control plane takes in appears as a StorageDevice.' },
  ],

  tasks: [
    {
      title: 'A discovery run for a growth',
      scenario: 'Write the run that probes only `worker-07` and `worker-08` and writes a growth document for the running cluster `prod`.',
      [`yaml`]: [
        'apiVersion: storage.simplyblock.io/v1alpha2',
        'kind: {{kind}}',
        'metadata:',
        '  name: prod-grow-2',
        '  namespace: simplyblock',
        'spec:',
        '  action: {{action}}',
        '  discover:',
        '    {{refField}}: prod',
        '    configName: prod-growth-2',
        '    {{namesField}}:',
        '      - worker-07',
        '      - worker-08',
      ].join('\n') + '\n',
      blanks: {
        kind: { accept: ['OperatorOps'], hint: 'the operator-level operation kind, short name `oops`' },
        action: { accept: ['Discover'], hint: 'its only action' },
        refField: { accept: ['clusterRef'], hint: 'copied into the draft so it grows rather than creates' },
        namesField: { accept: ['workers'], hint: 'names the machines to probe, exclusive with `nodeSelector`' },
      },
      explain: 'An `OperatorOps` with `action: Discover` writes the draft. `clusterRef` makes it a growth document without `spec.cluster`, and `workers` limits the probes to the named machines.',
    },
    {
      title: 'The approved growth document',
      scenario: 'Complete the document so that it adds `worker-08` to the zone `zone-b` of the running cluster `prod`, which has failure domains enabled, and so that the operator expands it.',
      [`yaml`]: [
        'apiVersion: storage.simplyblock.io/v1alpha2',
        'kind: {{kind}}',
        'metadata:',
        '  name: prod-growth-2',
        '  namespace: simplyblock',
        'spec:',
        '  approved: {{approved}}',
        '  {{refField}}: prod',
        '  nodeSets:',
        '    - name: growth-2',
        '      groups:',
        '        - name: zone-b',
        '          failureDomain: {{domain}}',
        '          workers: [worker-08]',
        '          mgmtInterface: br-ex',
        '          dataInterfaces: [br-ex]',
        '          devices:',
        '            {{deviceClass}}:',
        '              - "0000:c1:00.0"',
        '              - "0000:c2:00.0"',
      ].join('\n') + '\n',
      blanks: {
        kind: { accept: ['ClusterDeploymentConfig'], hint: 'growth is a second document of this kind, short name `cdc`' },
        approved: { accept: ['true'], hint: 'the review gate' },
        refField: { accept: ['clusterRef'], hint: 'names the existing cluster, in place of `cluster`' },
        domain: { accept: ['zone-b'], hint: 'the label the cluster already maps for that zone' },
        deviceClass: { accept: [`nvme`], hint: 'the device class the cluster is built of, as a group\'s device selection spells it' },
      },
      explain: 'A `ClusterDeploymentConfig` with `clusterRef` adds to the named cluster, and `approved: true` lets it expand. The cluster\'s failure-domain mode requires every group to carry its domain, and the devices are of the class the cluster is built of.',
    },
    {
      title: 'An explicit expansion request',
      scenario: 'Every node of the growth is `Online`. Write the one operation that asks the control plane to expand `prod`.',
      [`yaml`]: [
        'apiVersion: {{apiVersion}}',
        'kind: {{kind}}',
        'metadata:',
        '  name: prod-expand-2',
        '  namespace: simplyblock',
        'spec:',
        '  clusterRef: {{cluster}}',
        '  action: {{action}}',
      ].join('\n') + '\n',
      blanks: {
        apiVersion: { accept: ['storage.simplyblock.io/v1alpha2'], hint: 'the group and the served version' },
        kind: { accept: ['StorageClusterOps'], hint: 'the cluster-level operation kind, short name `scops`' },
        cluster: { accept: ['prod'], hint: 'the cluster that grew' },
        action: { accept: ['Expand'], hint: 'PascalCase, as every enum of the group' },
      },
      explain: 'A `StorageClusterOps` with `action: Expand` posts to the control plane\'s expand endpoint and waits for the cluster to read active. Nothing raises it, and it is written once, after the last new node is `Online`.',
    },
  ],
});
